"""Per-gene background prose for Panel B (aging/longevity). Results-independent."""

GENE_BG_B = {
"FOXO3": "FOXO3 is the most consistently replicated longevity-associated gene in human populations; protective alleles enrich in centenarians across ancestries. As a forkhead transcription factor it integrates insulin/IGF signalling with stress resistance, upregulating antioxidant, autophagy, and repair programmes when growth signalling is low. Its homologs extend lifespan in worms and flies, making it the bridge gene between the invertebrate and human longevity literatures.",
"SIRT1": "SIRT1 is an NAD-dependent deacetylase that couples metabolic state to chromatin and transcription. It deacetylates p53, FOXO factors, and PGC-1alpha, and its overexpression improves metabolic health in mammals, though lifespan effects are context-dependent. It is the most studied sirtuin and the canonical link between calorie restriction and gene regulation.",
"SIRT6": "SIRT6 is the sirtuin most directly tied to genome maintenance: it promotes double-strand-break repair, stabilises telomeric chromatin, and suppresses LINE1 retrotransposons. Sirt6-null mice age prematurely, and overexpression extends lifespan in males of some backgrounds. Among the panel it is the cleanest single-protein argument that repair quality sets aging pace.",
"MTOR": "The mTOR kinase is the central growth regulator of eukaryotes and the most pharmacologically validated aging target: rapamycin extends lifespan in yeast, worms, flies, and mice, the only drug-class effect of that breadth. MTOR integrates amino-acid, energy, and growth-factor inputs; its chronic activation drives the growth-first, maintenance-second physiology that aging theory blames for late-life decline.",
"IGF1": "Insulin-like growth factor 1 is the effector of the growth-hormone axis. Circulating IGF1 declines with age, but reduced IGF1 signalling extends lifespan in model organisms, a paradox resolved by distinguishing development-time growth promotion from adult maintenance suppression. Small, long-lived dog breeds and long-lived human kindreds sit at the low-signalling end.",
"IGF1R": "The IGF1 receptor transduces the IGF signal. Haploinsufficiency extends female lifespan in mice, and low IGF1R activity marks human longevity kindreds. It is one of the few receptors whose partial inhibition is life-extending, which makes it a reference point for the difference between blocking a pathway and tuning it.",
"INSR": "The insulin receptor is the entry point of the insulin signalling cascade. Its worm homolog daf-2 is the founding longevity gene of the modern field: reducing it doubles worm lifespan. INSR sits at the top of the most evolutionary conserved aging pathway known, which is why its handling in an ancient metazoan is intrinsically informative.",
"AKT1": "AKT1 is the central kinase of insulin/IGF signalling, phosphorylating FOXO factors to exclude them from the nucleus and activating mTOR signalling. In longevity terms it is the switch that decides between growth and maintenance. Its deep conservation makes it a near-certain find in any metazoan genome and a calibration point for the panel.",
"PTEN": "PTEN is the lipid phosphatase that opposes PI3K signalling, restraining the AKT-mTOR axis, and a major tumour suppressor. Subtle PTEN elevation extends mouse lifespan with improved metabolic health. It exemplifies the class of maintenance genes whose loss is catastrophic and whose mild gain is protective.",
"TP53": "p53 is the genome guardian: it senses DNA damage and forces arrest, senescence, or apoptosis. Aging biology's double bind is concentrated here: too little p53 activity yields cancer, too much yields stem-cell attrition and progeroid phenotypes. Cnidarian p53 homologs are ancient, and how a non-aging animal sets the p53 dial is a genuine open question.",
"CDKN2A": "CDKN2A encodes p16INK4a, the senescence brake whose expression rises steeply with age and serves as a standard biomarker of senescent cell burden. Clearing p16-positive cells extends healthspan in mice. Its evolutionary distribution is uneven: it is a relatively recent vertebrate innovation, so its absence from a cnidarian genome would be the expected, informative result.",
"ATM": "ATM is the apical kinase of the double-strand-break response; its loss causes ataxia-telangiectasia, with neurodegeneration, immunodeficiency, and cancer predisposition. ATM links DNA repair directly to neurology: the most radiosensitive human tissue phenotype includes the cerebellum. It is ancient and nearly universal in eukaryotes.",
"ATR": "ATR handles replication stress and single-stranded DNA, and its hypomorphic loss causes Seckel syndrome with microcephaly and progeroid features. Together with ATM it organises the checkpoint layer of genome maintenance. Its essentiality makes presence in any metazoan genome expected and its divergence pattern the informative variable.",
"BLM": "BLM is a RecQ helicase that dissolves recombination intermediates; biallelic loss causes Bloom syndrome, with genome instability, cancer predisposition, and growth failure. It is direct human genetic proof that helicase-based genome maintenance limits healthspan, and one of the clearest progeroid pathway genes.",
"WRN": "WRN is the RecQ helicase whose loss causes Werner syndrome, the canonical adult progeria: patients develop graying, cataracts, diabetes, atherosclerosis, and cancer by middle age. WRN is the strongest single-gene argument that genome-maintenance failure can phenocopy normal aging compressed in time, and its evolutionary history is notably patchy across animals.",
"NFE2L2": "NFE2L2 encodes NRF2, the master transcription factor of the antioxidant and electrophile response. NRF2 activity declines with age, its activation extends lifespan in models, and its pathway is the target of several approved and candidate drugs. In regeneration-competent animals the redox environment is actively managed; NRF2 is the likeliest transcription factor behind that management.",
"HSF1": "HSF1 is the heat-shock transcription factor, guardian of the proteome under stress. Its decline with age tracks the collapse of proteostasis, and its activation extends worm lifespan. It anchors the panel's proteostasis arm alongside autophagy, which matters doubly for the neurology connection because aggregation diseases are proteostasis failures.",
"ATG5": "ATG5 is core autophagy machinery, conjugating ATG12 in the pathway that builds autophagosomes. Neural-specific Atg5 loss causes neurodegeneration in mice without any other trigger, making it a direct genetic link between maintenance failure and neuronal death. It is deeply conserved and expected in any eukaryote.",
"ATG7": "ATG7 is the E1 enzyme activating both ATG12 and LC3 in autophagy. Its restoration in old mice improves healthspan, and its loss phenocopies aspects of accelerated aging. Like ATG5 it is expected to be universal in eukaryotes, so its copy number and divergence, not its presence, are the informative readouts.",
"BECN1": "Beclin-1 initiates autophagosome formation and connects autophagy to endosomal trafficking. A gain-of-function beclin variant extends mouse lifespan with reduced cardiac and renal pathology. BECN1 is also haploinsufficient-tumour-suppressive, placing it in the maintenance-genes-that-are-also-cancer-genes class that dominates the aging-cancer interface.",
"TFEB": "TFEB is the master regulator of lysosomal biogenesis and autophagic flux, shuttling to the nucleus when nutrients are scarce. Its overexpression clears aggregation-prone proteins in models of Alzheimer, Parkinson, and lysosomal storage disease, making it the panel's most direct link from longevity machinery to neurodegeneration therapy research.",
"PRKAA1": "PRKAA1 encodes the catalytic alpha1 subunit of AMPK, the cell's energy-depletion sensor. AMPK activation extends lifespan in worms and flies and is implicated in the effects of metformin and exercise. It is the metabolic complement of mTOR: where mTOR drives growth, AMPK enforces maintenance during scarcity.",
"GHR": "The growth hormone receptor anchors the somatotropic axis. Its inactivation produces Laron syndrome in humans, with marked protection from cancer and diabetes, and the longest-lived mouse on record is a GH-pathway mutant. GHR is a vertebrate-specific gene, so its expected absence from cnidarian genomes marks a boundary of the ancient maintenance toolkit.",
"APOE": "Apolipoprotein E is the strongest common genetic factor for both human longevity and Alzheimer disease: the e4 allele raises Alzheimer risk and shortens lifespan, e2 does the reverse. APOE is a vertebrate lipid-transport gene, expected to be absent from cnidarians; its role in this study is to mark, by contrast, which parts of the aging-neurology interface are evolutionary novelties.",
}
